        <section>
            <p>Aerosol performance is not defined only by formulation chemistry or valve design. The <strong>propellant
                    system</strong> is a core engineering variable that directly controls internal pressure, spray
                force, atomization quality, emptying behavior, and long-term product stability. Selecting the wrong
                propellant often results in weak spray, inconsistent discharge, phase separation, or regulatory
                non-compliance—especially in demanding climates such as India.</p>
            <p>This technical guide explains, using established aerosol science, how <strong>LPG, DME, HFCs, CO₂, and
                    N₂</strong> differ in physical behavior and how those differences translate into real-world aerosol
                performance. The focus is on vapor pressure curves, spray physics, and solvency effects—without
                speculation or marketing claims.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 id="why-propellant">Why Propellant Selection Is Critical in Aerosol Products</h2>
            <p>Propellants serve two fundamental functions in aerosols: they <strong>generate pressure</strong> and
                <strong>deliver mechanical energy</strong> to atomize the product through the valve and actuator. Unlike
                pumps or compressors, aerosol propellants rely on thermodynamic equilibrium inside a sealed container,
                making their physical properties central to system behavior.</p>
            <p>A change in propellant type can alter spray force, droplet size distribution, and discharge rate even
                when all other components remain unchanged. This is why propellant selection must be treated as an
                <strong>engineering decision</strong>, not a commodity choice.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Internal pressure stability over temperature</li>
                <li>Spray consistency from full to empty can</li>
                <li>Compatibility with formulation and packaging</li>
                <li>Compliance with flammability and environmental regulations</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 id="vapor-pressure">Understanding Vapor Pressure Curves</h2>
            <p>Vapor pressure defines the pressure exerted by a propellant in equilibrium with its liquid phase at a
                given temperature. In aerosols, vapor pressure determines the <strong>driving force behind spray
                    generation</strong>. Each propellant has a characteristic vapor pressure curve that dictates how
                pressure changes with temperature.</p>
            <p>Liquefied gas propellants maintain relatively constant pressure during discharge, while compressed gases
                show pressure decay as product is expelled. This difference explains many observed performance
                variations across propellant systems.</p>
